  "worked_solution": "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=2{e}^{-6x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=2.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{3-6x}={e}^{3}\\cdot {e}^{-6x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c={e}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=-{e}^{6x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $y={e}^{6x}$ is not a solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
